00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
tonight german chancellor olaf schultz  says that russia will face severe  economic and political consequences if  it invades ukraine now that warning came  as envoys from russia ukraine germany  and france convened today here in berlin  more attempts at diplomacy to avert a  war while more than a hundred thousand  russian troops remain positioned along  the border with ukraine  the numbers continue to grow  more troops more military hardware  and more big booms in video offered to  the media by the russian defense  ministry  [Applause]  and even if these big booms are only for  drills they are meant to be loud just as  russia's foreign ministry is being loud  and responding to overtures from the  west to turn the volume down  idealized approaches ultimatums threats  and lecturing  it's a path to nowhere  unfortunately many of our western  colleagues are now obsessed with this  form of public activity  i can't call it diplomacy  these ukrainian troops in the donbass  region were deployed at first to keep  russia-backed separatists at bay  now they're watching and waiting for  what they think will be an assault by  russian troops on the other side of the  border  we're ready and very motivated we know  who we're defending the ukrainian people  are behind us our home  we're ready to stand our ground until  the end  the donbass region though may not be the  main prize for russia as dw heard from a  chair of the european council on foreign  relations  i mean it's good that sort of berlin and  paris is now sort of accelerating  efforts on donbass on the means process  but we should understand that the  russian game at the moment is much  bigger uh donbass is a sideshow i have  to say  the russian game if you listen to mr  putin and see the documents that they've  put on the table is about ukraine as a  whole and it's about sort of changing  the entire european security order  as the u.s moves troops into romania to  bolster nato's border along the black  sea  carl biltz says it is in fact the u.s  and nato that russia most wants to deal  with  dw's chief international editor richard  walker spoke to latvia's prime minister  about how his country is dealing with  the russian buildup  prime minister carriage how dangerous is  the situation at the moment how close  are we to a possible war  well it's not clear  if there will or won't be a war but one  thing is clear president putin  is fighting against ukraine's  independence against their democracy  that's the threat that he feels  he talks about the encroachment of nato  but what he's really fighting is the  independence  of ukraine and this is what we in the  west what we in the european union and  what we as nato member states  have an obligation to support is  ukrainian independence that is what is  at stake but you do see the troops that  are amassed around ukraine as a real  threat a potential threat that could  lead to conflict of course it's a very  real threat we've seen the exercises  before last spring  much higher troop concentrations uh  there's a refusal to allow uh  observation of the exercises in belarus  which are also unannounced so there are  troops not only along the ukrainian  russian border but there are russian  troops now in belarus on the northern  border of ukraine as well now of course  latvia has a border with belarus itself  do you feel that there is a direct risk  to your country  my country is a member of nato as are  all three uh baltic countries so of  course we have uh as we speak today ten  nato member state uh militaries in  latvia there's the the german uh led uh  contingent of nato in lithuania the  british that contingent in estonia so we  don't feel a direct  military threat but certainly this is  very destabilizing for the entire region  and it changes the military posture  and we as nato member states need to  react i think it's very  important to strengthen nato's presence  all along the eastern flank that is  from the baltic down to the black sea  but especially of course also in the  baltic countries and this is where  germany is playing now an important role  germany has made the decision to  strengthen its presence in lithuania  which is very welcome news indeed now in  the discussion of trying to deter  vladimir putin from taking military  action there's a lot of focus on  sanctions possible sanctions and  nordstrom 2 is at the heart of that  debate wolf schultz the german  chancellor has refused to explicitly say  that he would put sanctions on  nordstream 2. what's your message to him  on that  the message is very simple everything  has to be on the table for it to be a  credible deterrent  putin has to understand that the  sanctions could bite in many different  ways so as long as nothing is taken off  of the table this is the way that we  have to move forward now you're a  long-standing critic of nordstream 2.  when you look at the current situation  do you feel that nordstrom 2 contributed  to this crisis  well we've been critics of north stream  one so all along and we have said from  the very beginning that such an undersea  a gas pipeline to germany will or could  at some point be used by russia as a  geopolitical  lever and we see that this is what the  kremlin is doing right now using gas  supplies to europe as a pressure with  reduced flows and thereby increasing  energy prices in europe  we thought it was a bad idea then and we  think it's a bad idea now for europe to  become too over dependent on energy  supplies from any one country especially  if that country is  russia and we know that gas promise is  state-controlled export monopoly it is  being used now as an economic tool  against uh the european union prime  minister current many thanks for your  time thank you  for more now i'm joined by sophia besh  she's an expert on european defense  policy at the center for  european reform it's good to see you  again let's let's talk about the  influence that um european allies such  as france and germany  have right now i mean do they have much  influence on what vladimir putin is  going to do  well that's a good question i mean  france and germany are obviously  influential powers in europe and they  are important voices in this crisis not  least because they would suffer under  economic sanctions should the conflict  get to that stage  and you know they have often cast  themselves as sort of mediating powers  in relations with russia germany has  historically often pursued the role of a  diplomatic mediator macron has  throughout his presidency attempted to  forge new negotiation paths with putin  and now they are trying to exert their  influence for instance as you said  through the normandy forum the benefits  of that forum is that we have ukraine  and russia both at the table  the hope is that we can use it to come  to a diplomatic solution revive the  so-called minsk accords that were struck  after  russia last invaded ukraine but they are  notoriously ambiguous they are  interpreted differently by all the  parties and they have never been fully  implemented in the end i think these  attacks can only succeed france and  germany can only succeed if russia  engages as a good faith participant  which is less than clear at this time  yeah that's a that's a very questionable  position at the moment we know the  biggest counterpart to putin is of  course u.s president joe biden  how much influence can france and  germany have on the white house at this  moment  well  the us and the europeans have been  really closely coordinated in this  crisis i would say um increasingly so no  one wants a war and as a western  alliance we have opted for this sort of  two-pronged strategy of deterrence on  the one hand and dialogue on the other  hand early on you know u.s and russia  have engaged in bilateral talks over  arms control issues and confidence  building measures and france and germany  have in recent days really increased the  intensity of their own diplomatic  efforts  you know we've had macron in moscow and  kiev we have schultz's conversations uh  with the leaders of the baltic states  today with poland's president and then  his planned trip to moscow i think those  conversations are important because  france and germany are really strongest  when they have the credibility and the  legitimacy of representing some sort of  broader european interest  and for that it's incredibly important  that they closely engage with and listen  to the interests and concerns of other  european states before they speak to  moscow or for that matter to to  washington yeah you know earlier this  week the french president emmanuel  macron he was in moscow to meet with  vladimir putin and it's important to  note that macron is  facing re-election in just two months  time he's been criticized for maybe  campaigning if you will  at the kremlin the german chancellor he  goes to the kremlin next week  should should he do anything differently  from what we saw macron do this week  charles is in a really difficult  position going to moscow because it's so  difficult to see what a diplomatic  off-ramp could look like at this point  and mccall faced the same issue you know  to your point whether he's campaigning  in the kremlin i think we've heard  throughout my presidency he has taken an  interest in relations with russia in  trying to re-engage the kremlin but it's  less and less clear what a compromise  could look like between western values  and russian interests at this point you  know russia seems to want to reinstate a  policy of spheres of influence in  europe's east denying a country like  ukraine the freedom to choose its own  alliances influence its broader politics  and foreign policy and europe and the us  are sort of sworn to protect the  decision-making autonomy and territorial  integrity of ukraine and any compromise  on the status of ukraine would  essentially undermine this this stance  of the west plus you know there is the  question of what message russia would  take away if it succeeded to essentially  blackmail us into a concession macron  did not get you know a silver bullet  deal when he went to moscow he did get  some tentative concessions on exercises  in batteries but we cannot be sure if  they're trustworthy and we heard the  nato section say today that the russian  militable that keeps intensifying the  crisis keeps  getting diceier and it seems  increasingly schultz's visit is becoming  a visit at the 11th hour you know that  the pressure is certainly on  sofia bash from the center for european  reform sophie as always we appreciate  your time and your insights thank you  thank you so much

